latest_plan = 'Based on our reflection, here\'s a step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Create a copy of the input grid to modify.\n\n2. Identify all non-zero colors present in the grid.\n\n3. For each non-zero color:\n   a. Find all connected regions of that color.\n   b. For each connected region:\n      i. Check if the region is enclosed (surrounded by either the same color or black).\n      ii. If enclosed:\n          - Determine the highest-valued color within the region or adjacent to it (excluding black).\n          - Fill the entire region with this highest-valued color.\n\n4. Implement a flood fill algorithm to perform the filling operation.\n\n5. Return the modified grid.\n\nHere\'s a more detailed breakdown of each step:\n\n1. Copy the input grid:\n   - Use the deep_copy() method of ColoredGrid.\n\n2. Identify non-zero colors:\n   - Iterate through the grid and collect unique non-zero color values.\n\n3. Process each color:\n   a. Use the find_connected_regions() method of ColoredGrid to get all regions for each color.\n   b. For each region:\n      i. Check enclosure:\n         - Examine all neighboring cells of the region.\n         - If all neighbors are either the same color, black, or out of bounds, the region is enclosed.\n      ii. If enclosed:\n          - Scan the region and its immediate neighbors to find the highest-valued color (excluding black).\n          - Use a flood fill algorithm to replace the entire region with this highest-valued color.\n\n4. Flood fill algorithm:\n   - Implement a recursive or iterative flood fill function that replaces all connected cells of the original color with the new color.\n\n5. Return the result:\n   - After processing all colors and regions, return the modified grid.\n\nThis plan addresses the generalized rule we identified: "Enclosed regions are filled with the highest-valued color that is either present within the region or adjacent to it, excluding black (0)." It should work for all the given test cases and any similar scenarios.'
